# security-and-hardening (Cursor)

## Threat model first

Before hardening: map trust boundaries (HTTP requests, form fields, file
uploads, webhooks, third-party APIs, message queues, and LLM output), name
the assets worth stealing/breaking, run STRIDE over each boundary
(Spoofing/Tampering/Repudiation/Information disclosure/DoS/Elevation of
privilege), and write abuse cases next to use cases. If you can't name the
trust boundaries for a feature, you're not ready to secure it.

## Always do (no exceptions)

Validate all external input at the boundary; parameterize every DB query;
encode output for XSS (use framework auto-escaping); HTTPS everywhere;
hash passwords with bcrypt/scrypt/argon2; set security headers (CSP, HSTS,
X-Frame-Options); httpOnly/secure/sameSite cookies for sessions; run the
package manager's native audit against the committed lockfile before
release.

## Ask first

New auth flows, storing new categories of sensitive data, new external
integrations, CORS changes, file upload handlers, rate-limit changes,
granting elevated permissions.

## Never do

Commit secrets; log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, full card numbers);
trust client-side validation as a security boundary; disable security
headers for convenience; use `eval()`/`innerHTML` with user data; store
auth tokens in localStorage; expose stack traces to users.

## Key prevention patterns

- **Injection**: parameterized queries/ORM, never string-concatenated SQL.
- **Auth**: bcrypt/argon2 hashing, httpOnly+secure+sameSite session
  cookies.
- **XSS**: framework auto-escaping; sanitize with DOMPurify if HTML must
  render raw.
- **Access control**: check both authentication AND resource ownership on
  every protected route.
- **SSRF**: for any server-side fetch of a user-influenced URL, allowlist
  scheme+host, resolve DNS and reject if any address is non-unicast
  (covers loopback, link-local metadata endpoint, private ranges), forbid
  redirects. Note the TOCTOU gap — pin the resolved IP for high-risk
  surfaces.
- **Input validation**: schema validation (Zod/similar) at every route
  boundary; file uploads restricted by type/size, don't trust the
  extension alone.

## Dependency audits

Native audit reachability triage: critical/high + reachable in
runtime/build/test/deploy paths → fix immediately; unreachable → fix soon,
not a blocker. Never run forced automated remediation
(`audit fix --force`) — preview, read changelogs, test each upgrade.
Verify registry signatures/provenance where supported. Find the real
install boundary/manager (workspace root owning the lockfile) before
trusting any audit; block install scripts by default, approve only the
minimum required packages.

## Securing AI/LLM features

Treat all model output as untrusted input — never straight into
`eval`/SQL/shell/`innerHTML`/file paths. Assume prompts can be hijacked —
the system prompt is not a security boundary, enforce permissions in code.
Keep secrets and other users' data out of the context window. Scope
tool/agent permissions to the minimum, require confirmation for
destructive actions. Cap tokens/rate/recursion depth. Partition vector
store embeddings per tenant in RAG.

## Secrets

`.env`/`.env.local` gitignored, `.env.example` committed as a template. If
a secret is ever committed, rotate it immediately — deleting the line or
rewriting history isn't enough, assume it's compromised.
